# React.js Best Practices

**glob**: `**/*.{jsx,tsx,js,ts}`

## Component Structure and Organization

- Use functional components with hooks instead of class components
- Keep components small and focused on a single responsibility
- Extract reusable logic into custom hooks
- Use named exports for components rather than default exports
- Group related components in dedicated folders
- Implement lazy loading for components when appropriate using React.lazy and Suspense

## State Management

- Use useState for local component state
- Prefer useReducer for complex state logic
- Keep state as close as possible to where it's used
- Use context API for sharing state across distant components
- Consider using state management libraries (Redux, Zustand, Jotai) only when necessary

## Performance Optimization

- Use React.memo for preventing unnecessary re-renders
- Implement useMemo for expensive calculations
- Use useCallback for functions passed to child components
- Avoid inline function definitions in render methods
- Use virtualization for long lists (react-window, react-virtualized)
- Implement code splitting to reduce initial bundle size

## Handling Side Effects

- Use useEffect for side effects
- Always define cleanup functions for subscriptions or event listeners
- Properly specify dependencies array in useEffect
- Use useLayoutEffect for DOM measurements that affect layout
- Avoid nested useEffect hooks

## Props and TypeScript

- Use TypeScript for type safety
- Define prop types using interfaces rather than type aliases
- Make props explicit and avoid passing unnecessary props
- Destructure props at the function parameter level
- Use default props for optional parameters
- Consider using prop spreading sparingly

## Styling Best Practices

- Use CSS-in-JS solutions or CSS Modules to avoid style conflicts
- Implement responsive designs with media queries
- Use design systems or component libraries for consistency
- Keep styling logic separate from business logic
- Consider accessibility in your styling (color contrast, focus states)

## Testing

- Write tests for components using Jest and React Testing Library
- Test component behavior rather than implementation details
- Use mock functions for external dependencies
- Test user interactions using fireEvent or user-event
- Implement snapshot testing for UI components

## Error Handling

- Implement error boundaries to catch and display errors
- Add fallback UI for error states
- Use try/catch blocks for async operations
- Provide meaningful error messages to users
- Log errors to monitoring services

## Forms

- Use controlled components for form elements
- Consider form libraries like Formik or React Hook Form for complex forms
- Implement proper form validation
- Use appropriate HTML5 input types and attributes
- Ensure form accessibility (labels, error messages, etc.)

## Accessibility

- Ensure proper semantic HTML elements
- Use appropriate ARIA attributes when necessary
- Ensure keyboard navigation works correctly
- Test with screen readers
- Implement proper focus management
- Ensure sufficient color contrast and text size

## Code Style and Conventions

- Follow consistent naming conventions
- Use ESLint and Prettier for code formatting
- Write descriptive comments and documentation
- Use absolute imports rather than relative when possible
- Keep the component folder structure consistent 